    </statBases>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- bed depth 4: what deep water leaves. Standable, pathCost 300, as RM_Channel_Superdeep; whether a pawn is held
       is the relative climb rule's call (RM_PocketGrid), and a pond with a shallow rim is walked out of (ruling 4). -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="NaturalTerrainBase">
    <defName>RM_Lakebed_Deep</defName>
    <label>deep dry lakebed</label>
    <description>The cracked floor of a drained deep pond, four levels below the surrounding ground. Where the bed shelves gently it can be walked out of; where it drops sheer, it holds whatever falls in. Water pumped back fills it again.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/FlowWorks/RM_Lakebed_Deep</texturePath>
    <renderPrecedence>330</renderPrecedence>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <pathCost>300</pathCost>
    <passability>Standable</passability>
    <natural>true</natural>
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <affordances>
      <li>Light</li>
    </affordances>
    <tags>
      <li>Soil</li>
    </tags>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>-4</Beauty>
    </statBases>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- POND_ELEVATION_BUILD_LAKES_RUN_DEEP: bed depth 8 and deeper, the middle of a big drained lake (RM_Volume.AbyssBedLevels).
       NO NEW ART: the deep lakebed texture, darkened by a vertex tint (TerrainDef.color feeds Graphic_Terrain), so the bowl's
       middle reads deeper than its shelves. Walking and climbing match RM_Lakebed_Deep; the relative climb rule (RM_PocketGrid)
       decides who is held, and a gradual bowl holds nobody. -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="NaturalTerrainBase">
    <defName>RM_Lakebed_Abyss</defName>
    <label>deep lake floor</label>
    <description>The cracked floor at the middle of a big drained lake, eight levels or more below the surrounding ground. The bed shelves up gently to the shore, so it can be walked out of. Water pumped back fills it again.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/FlowWorks/RM_Lakebed_Deep</texturePath>
    <color>(0.62, 0.58, 0.56)</color>
    <renderPrecedence>331</renderPrecedence>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <pathCost>300</pathCost>
    <passability>Standable</passability>
    <natural>true</natural>
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <affordances>
      <li>Light</li>
    </affordances>
    <tags>
      <li>Soil</li>
    </tags>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>-4</Beauty>
    </statBases>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       THE WET TIER LADDER — ruling 5.

       F is rendered as a TIER, not a percentage: trace/shallow →
       half/chest-deep → brimming/deep, off vanilla's own edge-aware ramp
       art. All three rungs are OURS.

       🔴 WHY TIER 1 IS NOT VANILLA ShallowFloodwater. Every temporary
       terrain the base game ships lives in Data/Odyssey — ShallowFloodwater
       and MarshFlood are both in Defs/Odyssey/TerrainDefs/Terrain_Temporary.xml,
       and Core's own Terrain_Water.xml:20 reaches them only through
       MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey". Naming one made this mod
       hard-require the DLC (FLOWWORKS_BUILD_PROGRAM_1 Phase 3). The temp
       terrain ENGINE is Core — TerrainGrid.SetTempTerrain refuses a
       non-temporary def and nothing else — so the fix is to ship our own
       temporary def, cloned from ShallowFloodwater's properties, and depend
       on no DLC at all.

       ALL of these are <temporary>true</temporary> — TerrainGrid.
       SetTempTerrain hard-refuses anything else, and temporary is what
       makes a release RECOVERABLE: the dug channel underneath survives and
       comes back when the liquid leaves (owner ruling 2026-09-02).

       Deliberately NO <tempTerrain><destroysFloors> — that flag MOVES the
       floor out of underGrid permanently and RemoveTempTerrain never puts
       it back, which is the exact damage that ruling removed.

       Ruling 26: fill NEVER captures. A brimming deep canal is a tax, not
       a barrier — so tier 3 is costly (300, vanilla WaterDeep's number)
       but STANDABLE, unlike vanilla WaterDeep which is Impassable.
       ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- Tier 1 of 3 — a trace of liquid, read as shallow. A property-for-
       property clone of Odyssey's ShallowFloodwater (ParentName WaterShallow,
       temporary, the Flood tag, canFreeze false, an empty driesTo so a
       moisture pump cannot turn temp terrain into stony soil) with none of
       its DLC gating. Same parent, so the edge-aware WaterShallowRamp art,
       the wade cost, extinguishesFire and the water depth shader all come
       free and it reads identically in play. -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="WaterShallow">
    <defName>RM_Fill_Water_Trace</defName>
    <label>shallow water</label>
    <description>Water standing ankle-to-knee deep. Wading it is easy.</description>
    <!-- DEPTH_FILL_COST_MATRIX_1: a trace only occurs at D=3 (FillTier), so it must beat dry Deep 80. PROVISIONAL. -->
    <pathCost>90</pathCost>
    <temporary>true</temporary>
    <driesTo></driesTo>
    <canFreeze>false</canFreeze>
    <renderPrecedence>394</renderPrecedence>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <li>WaterFreshShallow</li>
      <li>WaterFreshShallowStill</li>
      <li>Flood</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- Tier 2 of 3 — half full, read as chest-deep. -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="WaterShallow">
    <defName>RM_Fill_Water_Half</defName>
    <label>chest-deep water</label>
    <description>Water standing chest-deep. Wading it is slow work.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterChestDeepRamp</texturePath>
    <pollutedTexturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterChestDeepRampPolluted</pollutedTexturePath>
    <temporary>true</temporary>
    <driesTo></driesTo>
    <canFreeze>false</canFreeze>
    <!-- DEPTH_FILL_COST_MATRIX_1: was 42, cheaper than dry Mid 45 (a half-full mid canal was FASTER than
         a dry one). Half occurs at D=2 and D=3, so it must beat dry Deep 80. PROVISIONAL. -->
    <pathCost>120</pathCost>
    <extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>30</extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>240</extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <renderPrecedence>395</renderPrecedence>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <li>WaterFreshShallow</li>
      <li>WaterFreshShallowStill</li>
      <li>Flood</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- Tier 3 of 3 — brimming, read as deep. Standable per ruling 26. -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="WaterShallow">
    <defName>RM_Fill_Water_Brim</defName>
    <label>deep water</label>
    <description>Water standing deep enough to swim. Crossing it is punishing, but anyone can stand in it.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterDeepRamp</texturePath>
    <pollutedTexturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterDeepRampPolluted</pollutedTexturePath>
    <temporary>true</temporary>
    <driesTo></driesTo>
    <canFreeze>false</canFreeze>
    <pathCost>300</pathCost>
    <extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>60</extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>400</extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <renderPrecedence>396</renderPrecedence>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <li>WaterFreshShallow</li>
      <li>WaterFreshDeep</li>
      <li>Flood</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- A FLOODED SUPERDEEP cell. Still its own tier rather than tier 3,
       because the temp layer wins in TerrainAt and a flooded superdeep
       cell must keep reading as superdeep — but PASSABLE now, matching
       RM_Channel_Superdeep. 🔑 RULING 26 IS EXACTLY THIS: "SUPERDEEP
       captures as if dry" — fill changes nothing about whether a pawn
       falls in, so dry and flooded must have the same passability or the
       fill would silently become the barrier the ruling says it is not. -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="WaterShallow">
    <defName>RM_Fill_Water_Superdeep</defName>
    <label>very deep water</label>
    <description>Water filling a very deep pit. Whatever falls in is held there, and the water goes to work on it.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterDeepRamp</texturePath>
    <pollutedTexturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterDeepRampPolluted</pollutedTexturePath>
    <temporary>true</temporary>
    <driesTo></driesTo>
    <canFreeze>false</canFreeze>
    <pathCost>300</pathCost>
    <passability>Standable</passability>
    <renderPrecedence>397</renderPrecedence>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <li>WaterFreshDeep</li>
      <li>Flood</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       THE TAR TIER LADDER — FLOWWORKS_BUILD_PROGRAM_1 Phase 7, his ruling
       "Tar needs the viscosity most of all."

       Same shape as the water ladder above (ParentName WaterShallow,
       temporary, empty driesTo, canFreeze false), plus extinguishesFire
       false (tar burns) and a near-black tint so the TINTED-VANILLA
       FALLBACK reads as tar rather than as plain water even before Alpha
       Biomes' real art is adopted — §13's named disease ("tar rippling
       like water") is a colour problem here, not a motion one; the ripple
       animation itself is WaterShallow/Deep's own shader and is not fixed
       by this fallback. Alpha Biomes' own `AB_Tar` proves the only donor
       tar water-ramp texture is `AB_TarDeepRamp` (there is no shallow
       variant — AB's own `AB_ArtificialTar` "tar puddle" reuses the same
       deep ramp), so all four tiers below adopt that ONE texture uniformly
       once Alpha Biomes is present; see
       Patches/Canals/RM_Fluid_Tar_AlphaBiomesAdoption.xml for the
       PatchOperationConditional that swaps it in, gated on `sarg.
       alphabiomes` (verified against that mod's own About.xml) exactly the
       way ManyWaters already gates RM_Slime_* on AB_SlimeRamp.

       pathCost per tier is anchored to Alpha Biomes' own real tar-ground
       costs (AB_TarMud 100, AB_ArtificialTar 200) rather than invented,
       topping out at vanilla WaterDeep's 300 ceiling every other tier-3
       terrain in this mod already uses. -->

  <!-- Tier 1 of 3 — a trace of tar, read as shallow but already thick. -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="WaterShallow">
    <defName>RM_Fill_Tar_Trace</defName>
    <!-- burnable fill: WaterShallow's extinguishesFire put a burning occupant out at once (ruling 22: one held
         in a burning pit always catches). Live 2026-10-05: oil-pit pirate took heatstroke, never a burn. -->
    <extinguishesFire>false</extinguishesFire>
    <label>shallow tar</label>
    <description>Tar standing ankle-to-knee deep. Wading it is easy. Black and patient, it sucks at every step.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterShallowRamp</texturePath>
    <temporary>true</temporary>
    <driesTo></driesTo>
    <canFreeze>false</canFreeze>
    <color>(86,82,88)</color>
    <pathCost>100</pathCost>
    <renderPrecedence>394</renderPrecedence>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <li>WaterFreshShallow</li>
      <li>WaterFreshShallowStill</li>
      <li>Flood</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- Tier 2 of 3 — half full, read as chest-deep. -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="WaterShallow">
    <defName>RM_Fill_Tar_Half</defName>
    <!-- burnable fill: WaterShallow's extinguishesFire put a burning occupant out at once (ruling 22: one held
         in a burning pit always catches). Live 2026-10-05: oil-pit pirate took heatstroke, never a burn. -->
    <extinguishesFire>false</extinguishesFire>
    <label>chest-deep tar</label>
    <description>Tar standing chest-deep. Wading it is slow work. Black and patient, it sucks at every step.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterChestDeepRamp</texturePath>
    <pollutedTexturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterChestDeepRampPolluted</pollutedTexturePath>
    <temporary>true</temporary>
    <driesTo></driesTo>
    <canFreeze>false</canFreeze>
    <color>(86,82,88)</color>
    <pathCost>200</pathCost>
    <extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>60</extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>320</extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <renderPrecedence>395</renderPrecedence>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <li>WaterFreshShallow</li>
      <li>WaterFreshShallowStill</li>
      <li>Flood</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- Tier 3 of 3 — brimming, read as deep. Standable per ruling 26. -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="WaterShallow">
    <defName>RM_Fill_Tar_Brim</defName>
    <!-- burnable fill: WaterShallow's extinguishesFire put a burning occupant out at once (ruling 22: one held
         in a burning pit always catches). Live 2026-10-05: oil-pit pirate took heatstroke, never a burn. -->
    <extinguishesFire>false</extinguishesFire>
    <label>deep tar</label>
    <description>Tar standing deep enough to swim. Crossing it is punishing, but anyone can stand in it. Black and patient, it sucks at every step.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterDeepRamp</texturePath>
    <pollutedTexturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterDeepRampPolluted</pollutedTexturePath>
    <temporary>true</temporary>
    <driesTo></driesTo>
    <canFreeze>false</canFreeze>
    <color>(86,82,88)</color>
    <pathCost>300</pathCost>
